+# Data Model: Accept the MCP Standard Metadata Field in Handoff Tool Calls
+
+**Feature**: 001-bench | **Date**: 2026-10-26
+
+Phase 1 of `/speckit.plan`. This feature has **no persistent data store**; its "model" is the shape of the inbound `tools/call` request envelope and the closed-set validation that decides acceptance before dispatch. The entities below are wire shapes and their rules, extracted from the spec's Key Entities and confirmed against `task_handoff.py`.
+
+## Entity summary
+
+| Entity | Kind | Persisted? | Changed by this feature? |
+|--------|------|-----------|--------------------------|
+| `ToolCallRequest` | inbound JSON-RPC `params` object for `tools/call` | No (transient frame) | Yes — `_meta` becomes an accepted optional key |
+| `StandardMetadataObject` | value of `params._meta` | No | Yes — now explicitly validated as an object when present, otherwise opaque |
+| `AdapterIdentity` | process-bound `(host, session)` from environment | No (process config) | No — unchanged; still not overridable by a caller |
+| `ToolArgumentSchema` | per-tool closed set of allowed/required argument keys | No | No — unchanged (source of the identity-override refusal) |
+
+## ToolCallRequest
+
+The `params` object of a `tools/call` request. It must be a JSON object. Its keys form a **closed set**.
+
+### Fields
+
+| Field | Type | Required | Accepted keys rule | Notes |
+|-------|------|----------|--------------------|-------|
+| `name` | string | Present in practice; dispatch requires it to be one of `{offer, receive, status}` or the call fails inside dispatch with "unknown handoff tool" | member of closed set `{name, arguments, _meta}` | Not interpreted here; selected by dispatch. |
+| `arguments` | object (JSON object) | Optional; if absent/`null`, dispatch treats it as `{}` | member of closed set `{name, arguments, _meta}` | Must be an object when non-null, else dispatch raises "tool arguments must be an object". |
+| `_meta` | **object (JSON object)** | **Optional** | member of closed set `{name, arguments, _meta}` | **Accepted by this feature.** When present, MUST be an object (a mapping). Contents are opaque: not read, not forwarded, not executed. Absent → fine. Present-and-not-an-object (string/number/list/bool/`null`) → rejected. |
+
+### Validation rules (the pre-dispatch guard, evaluated in order)
+
+1. `params` is a JSON object, else refuse (`tools/call params must be an object`). *(unchanged)*
+2. Closed set: no key outside `{name, arguments, _meta}` may appear, else refuse (`tools/call params contain an unsupported field`). *(changed: `_meta` added to the permitted set; every other unknown key still refused — FR-003)*
+3. `_meta` type: if the key `_meta` is present, its value MUST be an object, else refuse (`tools/call metadata must be an object`). *(added — FR-002; covers string/number/list/bool/null)*
+4. Dispatch proceeds with only `(name, arguments, host, session)`. `name` must be a known tool and `arguments` must satisfy the tool's schema; failures here surface as a tool-level error, independent of metadata handling. *(unchanged)*
+
+All refusals in steps 1–3 map to JSON-RPC error `-32602 invalid request` and occur **before** any tool runs, so they cause **no Runtime side effect** (no HTTP to the Runtime).
+
+### Relationships
+
+- `ToolCallRequest` **has at most 1** `StandardMetadataObject` (the `_meta` value).
+- `ToolCallRequest` names exactly one of the three tools via `name` (or none, if the call is refused before dispatch).
+- Every request is evaluated against the single process-scoped `AdapterIdentity`.
+
+## StandardMetadataObject
+
+The optional per-call metadata a conforming MCP client attaches (e.g. `{"progressToken": ...}`).
+
+### Fields / structure
+
+- Value type constraint: **object (mapping)** only. Any other JSON type is invalid.
+- Contents: **opaque**. The adapter performs no interpretation beyond confirming it is an object. Nested values of any shape are allowed and ignored.
+- Lifecycle: exists for the duration of one frame; never carried across frames, never written to a projection/event, never forwarded to the Runtime.
+
+### Rules
+
+- Absent → accepted (dispatch unaffected).
+- Present + object (including `{}`) → accepted (dispatch unaffected). SC-001 / FR-001.
+- Present + non-object (string/number/list/bool/`null`) → rejected before dispatch. SC-002 / FR-002.
+
+## AdapterIdentity
+
+The host/session identity the server process is configured with; it fixes who is acting.
+
+### Fields
+
+| Source | Env var | Validator | Overridable by caller? |
+|--------|---------|-----------|------------------------|
+| host | `GRAPHHELM_MCP_HOST` | `PORTABLE_HOST` regex via `_host()` at startup | No |
+| session | `GRAPHHELM_SESSION_ID` | `ID` regex via `_id()` at startup; cross-checked in `_session` per dispatch | No |
+
+### Relationship & rule
+
+- One `AdapterIdentity` per running process; shared by all frames on that connection.
+- **Identity-override refusal (FR-004, unchanged):** because the per-tool `ToolArgumentSchema` does not include host/session/identity keys, passing them inside `arguments` trips the existing `set(arguments) - allowed` check and is refused. Adding `_meta` to the *params* closed set does not change the *arguments* closed set, so this refusal is preserved verbatim.
+
+## ToolArgumentSchema (per tool, from `_mcp_tools`)
+
+Unchanged by this feature; listed so the model is complete and the identity-override rule is grounded.
+
+| Tool | Allowed argument keys | Required argument keys |
+|------|----------------------|------------------------|
+| `offer` | `recipientHost`, `recipientSessionId`, `handoffId` | `recipientHost`, `recipientSessionId` |
+| `receive` | `offerId` | `offerId` |
+| `status` | `offerId` | — (none) |
+
+Any other key in `arguments` (including any identity/host/session key) → refused ("tool arguments contain an unsupported field").
+
+## State transitions
+
+This feature introduces **no new persistent state machine**. The only state change in scope is the **pre-dispatch decision**, which is a pure function of the request:
+
+```
+                       +-------------------------------------------+
+   tools/call frame --> | 1. params is an object?                   |  no  -> -32602 (no tool runs)
+                       +-------------------------------------------+
+                                     yes
+                       +-------------------------------------------+
+                       | 2. keys subset of {name,arguments,_meta}? |  no  -> -32602 (no tool runs)   [FR-003]
+                       +-------------------------------------------+
+                                     yes
+                       +-------------------------------------------+
+                       | 3. if _meta present, is it an object?     |  no  -> -32602 (no tool runs)   [FR-002]
+                       +-------------------------------------------+
+                                     yes
+                       +-------------------------------------------+
+                       | 4. dispatch(name, arguments, identity)    |      [FR-001: _meta invisible]
+                       +-------------------------------------------+
+                            |                         |
+                     known tool + valid args      unknown tool / bad args
+                            v                         v
+                 result.isError = false        result.isError = true (tool-level; may touch Runtime)
+
+   Oversized / unterminated frame --> -32600 BEFORE json.loads (framed size > MAX_MCP_MESSAGE)   [FR-005]
+```
+
+The downstream recorded-state outcomes (`recorded` / `already_recorded` returned by `offer`/`receive`, and the status listing) are **out of scope** and byte-for-byte unchanged; metadata cannot reach them because dispatch receives only `name` and `arguments`.
+
+## Invariants preserved (cross-referenced)
+
+- Closed-set strictness except for `_meta` (FR-003).
+- Object-only metadata (FR-002); metadata is dispatch-neutral (FR-001 / SC-001 / SC-004).
+- Identity cannot be overridden through arguments (FR-004).
+- Frame-size bound precedes all parameter inspection (FR-005).
+- Requests without `_meta` behave identically to before (SC-004): for such requests, the step-2 set difference equals the old one and step 3 is skipped.
