+# Implementation Plan: Accept the MCP Standard Metadata Field in Handoff Tool Calls
+
+**Branch**: `main` | **Date**: 2026-10-26 | **Spec**: [spec.md](./spec.md)
+
+**Input**: Feature specification from `/specs/001-bench/spec.md`
+
+**Note**: This plan is the output of the `/speckit.plan` command. It fills the `.specify/templates/plan-template.md` structure and is followed by the Phase 0 artifact `research.md`, the Phase 1 artifacts `data-model.md`, `contracts/`, and `quickstart.md`.
+
+## Summary
+
+The task-handoff MCP stdio adapter (`plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py`, run as `task_handoff.py --mcp-stdio`) rejects every `tools/call` whose `params` object carries any key other than `name` and `arguments` (a one-set-difference guard at the `tools/call` branch of `_mcp_stdio`). Native, conforming MCP clients attach the standard per-call `_meta` object (e.g. `{"progressToken": ...}`), so every handoff tool fails before dispatch. The technical approach is a **minimal, additive relaxation** of that single guard: admit `_meta` into the closed set of allowed `params` keys, and add one companion check that, when `_meta` is present, its value is a JSON object. Only `params.get("name")` and `params.get("arguments")` are forwarded to dispatch, so `_meta` is structurally ignored for routing and argument interpretation. Every other guardrail (unknown-field refusal, identity-override refusal, oversized-frame refusal) is untouched because the edit adds keys to a permitted set rather than widening the other checks. The identical edit is applied byte-for-byte to the Codex companion copy (`plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/task_handoff.py`) so the two shipped adapters remain byte-identical, and one dedicated `_meta` case is added to `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/test_task_handoff.py` exercising the acceptance alongside the existing rejection cases in the same suite.
+
+## Technical Context
+
+**Language/Version**: Python, CPython **3.11.9** (measured on this machine; `python --version`). Standard library only. The adapter already runs under `from __future__ import annotations` and PEP 604 hints, consistent with 3.11.
+
+**Primary Dependencies**: None new. The module uses only the standard library (`json`, `os`, `sys`, `hashlib`, `argparse`, `urllib.parse`, `datetime`, and the sibling `session_hook` module). No third-party package, MCP SDK, or framework is introduced and none is required.
+
+**Storage**: N/A for this change. Validation is in-memory. Dispatch reads a bearer-token file and talks to the Runtime over loopback HTTP, but the relaxation happens entirely *before* any dispatch/Runtime call, so no persistence is touched by the accepted/rejected decision itself.
+
+**Testing**: Python standard-library **`unittest`** (the existing suite `test_task_handoff.py` uses `unittest.TestCase`, subprocess, and a local `ThreadingHTTPServer` on loopback). Driven via `python -m unittest`. No pytest dependency exists in this plugin. Tests require no internet, Docker, credentials, provider account, browser, or production infrastructure — loopback only.
+
+**Target Platform**: Windows (this machine; PowerShell 5.1; CPython 3.11.9 64-bit). Cross-platform by construction: pure standard library, with POSIX/Windows branches already present in `session_hook` (`msvcrt` vs `fcntl`).
+
+**Project Type**: stdio MCP server / CLI hook shipped as a host plugin (Python). It is one of two plugins (`graphhelm`, `graphhelm-codex-hooks`) that intentionally bundle the identical `task_handoff.py`.
+
+**Performance Goals**: N/A beyond what already exists. There is no hot path; the change is O(1) set-difference plus an `in` + `isinstance` per `tools/call`. Frames are bounded by `MAX_MCP_MESSAGE = 32 KiB` (`readline(MAX_MCP_MESSAGE + 1)`). No new scaling target.
+
+**Constraints**:
+- Offline: the reaching test must not need network beyond `127.0.0.1`.
+- No execution during validation: the `_meta` contents are never executed, deserialized-for-effect, or forwarded; only its JSON type (object vs not) is checked.
+- Bounded frame: the oversized-frame refusal (size > `MAX_MCP_MESSAGE`) takes precedence and fires before any `params` inspection.
+- Byte-identity: the primary and Codex companion adapters must stay byte-for-byte identical (SC-003 / FR-006).
+
+**Scale/Scope**: Tiny and local. One logical function body in one shared ~544-line file, duplicated verbatim into two plugin directories, plus one added test case in one existing test file. Nothing else in the tree is touched.
+
+## Constitution Check
+
+*GATE: Must pass before Phase 0 research. Re-checked after Phase 1 design.*
+
+> **Governance substitution (recorded per command guidance):** No project constitution file exists in this checkout (there is no `memory/constitution.md` / `.specify/memory/constitution.md`). Per the command, the repository-root `AGENTS.md` is used as the governance reference. Many `AGENTS.md` invariants target the Rust Graph Kernel workspace (`core/*`, `apps/cli`) and do not directly bind a Python hook plugin; the gates below are the subset that genuinely applies to this change. Items marked (informational) are noted but not gating.
+
+| # | Gate (source) | Requirement | Applies? | Status | Justification |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Documentation language (AGENTS.md "Documentation language") | All new/edited documentation is English | Yes | PASS | All artifacts under `specs/001-bench/` are written in English; no Portuguese/non-English prose. |
+| 2 | Smallest adequate observer (JPD "Proof rules"; Keel) | Prove the promise with the smallest observer; name the contract a test would catch | Yes | PASS | The observer is a dedicated `_meta` case in the existing contract suite plus the byte-identity hash check; both reach exactly the touched behavior. See `quickstart.md`. |
+| 3 | No ceremonial scaffold (JPD; "Repository boundaries") | No TODO/TBD/unimplemented!, no empty handlers, no unused modules/stubs | Yes | PASS | The change extends an existing branch with two checks; no new module, type, helper, or stub is introduced. |
+| 4 | Treat input as untrusted; bound before work (AGENTS.md "Security rules") | Repository/DSL/MCP frames treated as untrusted; bounded size; no execution during validation | Yes | PASS | The relaxation type-checks `_meta` only as "is it an object"; its contents are opaque and never executed, forwarded, or parsed for effect. Frame bound precedes parameter inspection. |
+| 5 | Deterministic enforcement (Constitutional invariants; JPD) | Deterministic code enforces transitions; no dependence on time/randomness/iteration order/locale | Yes | PASS | The guard is a pure set difference plus `in`/`isinstance`; fully deterministic. Tests use fixed env vars and fixed IDs. |
+| 6 | No new external dependency (Keel objective contract; Security) | A new dependency is named in the PR body | Yes | PASS | Zero new dependencies (stdlib only) — nothing to name. |
+| 7 | Secrets hygiene (AGENTS.md "Security rules") | No secrets in DSL/fixtures/logs/crash output | Yes | PASS | The `_meta` test case embeds no secret; existing tests use a fixed `"test-token"`. No fixture golden values change. |
+| 8 | Proportional card (Keel card table) | Bounded code change → three-line card; behavioral evidence required for security-relevant validation | Yes | PASS | Scope = 2 files (twin `task_handoff.py`) + 1 test case; promise = "accept object-valued `_meta`, keep all other refusals"; proof command = the plugin contract suite. Card recorded in "Project Structure" below. |
+| 9 | Byte-identical deployed adapters (spec FR-006 / SC-003; repo twin-file convention) | Primary and Codex companion `task_handoff.py` remain byte-identical | Yes | PASS (design) | Same literal edit applied to both; verified post-change by equal SHA-256 (see `quickstart.md`). Noted as a hard acceptance gate. |
+| 10 | Delivery process (AGENTS.md "Delivery: issue, review, merge") | Issue-first, identity line, one review by a non-author lane, PR content, closing-keyword check, merge = delivery | Informational | PASS (deferred to delivery) | Process gate satisfied at PR/merge time (not at plan time). No closing keyword is written beside an issue this plan does not intend to close. |
+| 11 | Rust workspace gate / core crate boundaries (AGENTS.md "Toolchain", "Repository boundaries") | `cargo fmt/clippy/test`, crate layout, no circular deps | Informational | N/A (out of scope) | `plugins/` is **not** a member of the root cargo workspace (verified: `Cargo.toml` lists no `plugins/*`). The change is Python and outside the Rust kernel; the Rust gate neither builds nor observes it. The reaching observer is the Python contract suite, per item 2. |
+
+**GATE RESULT**: PASS. No unjustified violations, therefore the **Complexity Tracking** table below is left empty (no violations to justify).
+
+## Project Structure
+
+### Documentation (this feature)
+
+```text
+specs/001-bench/
+├── plan.md              # This file (command output)
+├── research.md          # Phase 0 output (decisions, rationale, alternatives)
+├── data-model.md        # Phase 1 output (entities, fields, validation, state)
+├── quickstart.md        # Phase 1 output (runnable validation guide)
+├── contracts/           # Phase 1 output
