+# Quickstart Validation: Accept `_meta` in Handoff Tool Calls
+
+**Feature**: 001-bench | **Date**: 2026-10-26 | **Type**: runnable validation guide (not implementation)
+
+Phase 1 of `/speckit.plan`. These are the runnable scenarios that prove the feature end-to-end from this checkout. Field/decision semantics are in [`./data-model.md`](./data-model.md); the wire shapes and worked request/response examples are in [`./contracts/mcp-stdio-tools-call.md`](./contracts/mcp-stdio-tools-call.md). Implementation steps themselves belong in `tasks.md`.
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+- CPython 3.11.x available as `python` (measured here: **3.11.9**, `C:\...\WindowsApps\python.exe`). No `py` launcher is required.
+- No third-party packages — the adapter and its tests use only the standard library.
+- Offline. The observer uses loopback (`127.0.0.1`) only; no internet, Docker, credentials, provider account, or browser.
+- Run commands from the **repository root** (the directory containing `plugins/` and `.specify/`).
+
+> **PowerShell exit-code caveat (observed).** On PowerShell 5.1, redirecting a native process's stderr (`python ... 2>&1`) makes PowerShell treat unittest's stderr progress lines as error records and can report a spurious non-zero exit even when the suite is green. Treat the summary line (`Ran N tests ... OK`) as authoritative, or run without `2>&1`. This mirrors the repo-wide rule to read the gate output rather than trust a piped exit code.
+
+## Scenario A — The two adapters stay byte-identical (FR-006 / SC-003)
+
+**Promise**: the primary and Codex companion adapters are byte-for-byte identical after the edit.
+
+**Command** (PowerShell):
+```powershell
+Get-FileHash plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py,plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/task_handoff.py -Algorithm SHA256 | Format-Table Hash,Path -AutoSize
+```
+Equivalent (`git`, shows the object blob id):
+```bash
+git hash-object plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/task_handoff.py
+```
+
+**Expected**: **two identical hashes** (one per file; the SHA-256 differs from the pre-change `2AEAE452...` only because the content changed — the point is the two files now agree with each other). If they differ, a twin drifted and SC-003 fails.
+
+## Scenario B — Full contract suite is green, including the new `_meta` case (FR-008; proves FR-001..FR-005)
+
+**Promise**: the relaxation accepts an object-valued `_meta` and every other guardrail (unknown field, non-object `_meta`/null, identity override, oversized frame) still refuses with no Runtime side effect; the whole suite passes.
+
+**Command**:
+```bash
+python -m unittest plugins.graphhelm.hooks.test_task_handoff
+```
+(On Windows PowerShell: `python -m unittest plugins.graphhelm.hooks.test_task_handoff` — do **not** rely on `$?`/`$LASTEXITCODE` when combined with `2>&1`; read the summary.)
+
+**Expected**: a final summary line of the form `Ran <N> tests in <t>s` followed by `OK`, with `<N>` equal to the 9 pre-existing cases **plus** the new dedicated `_meta` acceptance case (i.e. `>= 10`), and **zero failures/errors**. The new case drives the real `--mcp-stdio` server through subprocess, sends a `tools/call` whose `params` carry `name`, `arguments`, and an object-valued `_meta` (e.g. `{"progressToken": ...}`), and asserts a successful result whose shape equals the no-metadata equivalent — the same observable boundary the other rejection cases use.
+
+**Why this is the observer**: it exercises the actual guard (not a reimplementation), runs in the same process/suite as the rejection cases (per FR-008), and, because refused calls return before dispatch, the negative cases can assert no Runtime request was made. Note the Rust cargo gate / `./ci/gate.ps1` is **not** the observer for this change: `plugins/` is not a member of the root cargo workspace.
+
+## Scenario C — Manual stdio smoke of the NEW refusal band (FR-002, optional; no live Runtime needed)
+
+**Purpose**: an independent, self-contained check that a *present-but-non-object* `_meta` is refused with `-32602` before dispatch, with no HTTP to the Runtime. This only needs the server to get past startup (valid binding env vars); a params-level refusal never reads the token file or calls the Runtime.
+
+**Setup (PowerShell)**:
+```powershell
+$env:GRAPHHELM_MCP_HOST      = "codex"
+$env:GRAPHHELM_SESSION_ID    = "receiver-1"
+$env:GRAPHHELM_EXECUTION_ID  = "run-1"
+$env:GRAPHHELM_TOKEN_FILE    = "run-1"            # non-empty ID string; content not read on this path
+$env:GRAPHHELM_RUNTIME_URL   = "http://127.0.0.1:8791"
+```
+
+**Drive (PowerShell)** — one frame with `_meta` a string (must refuse):
+```powershell
+'{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":6,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"status","arguments":{},"_meta":"oops"}}' |
+  python plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py --mcp-stdio
+```
+
+**Expected**: exactly one stdout line,
+```json
+{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":6,"error":{"code":-32602,"message":"invalid request"}}
+```
+Substitute `_meta` values `"7"`, `true`, `[1]`, and `null` (e.g. `"...,"_meta":null}`) — each yields the same `-32602`. A control with `"bogus":1` added alongside a valid `_meta` also yields `-32602` (unknown-field guardrail, FR-003).
+
+> The **accepted** object-`_meta` path is best proven by Scenario B, because dispatching `status` there contacts the local loopback Runtime that the test harness starts; driving it manually would require that server. Both bands share the same guard, and Scenario B covers the acceptance while Scenario C independently covers the refusal with no external dependency.
+
+## Scenario D — Baseline sanity (optional, to confirm no drift before implementing)
+
+Re-running Scenario B **before** touching code should show the original `Ran 9 tests ... OK`. This is the red/green anchor: implementers can confirm the starting state is green, add the `_meta` handling + case, and re-run to green at `>= 10`.
+
+## Requirement → observer map
+
+| Req / Success criterion | Proven by | Observable expectation |
+|-------------------------|-----------|------------------------|
+| FR-001 / SC-001 (metadata dispatched as if absent, all tools) | B (+ C control for shape) | `result.isError:false`; result text equals no-metadata equivalent |
+| FR-002 / SC-002 (non-object / null `_meta` refused pre-dispatch) | B, C | `-32602`; zero Runtime requests |
+| FR-003 / SC-002 (other unknown fields still refused) | B, C | `-32602` on unknown key, with or without valid `_meta` |
+| FR-004 (identity override through args refused) | B (existing case) | `result.isError:true`, unchanged behavior |
+| FR-005 / SC-002 (oversized frame refused before parse) | B (existing cases) | single `-32600`; non-zero exit; no second parse; no Runtime work |
+| FR-006 / SC-003 (byte-identical twins) | A | equal SHA-256 for both `task_handoff.py` |
+| FR-007 / SC-004 (no behavior change without `_meta`) | B (all 9 prior cases stay green) | identical responses for handshake/ping/list/plain calls |
+| FR-008 (dedicated in-suite `_meta` case; whole suite passes) | B | `Ran >= 10 tests ... OK`, zero failures/errors |
+
+## Done means
+
+- Scenario A prints two equal hashes.
+- Scenario B prints `OK` with the new `_meta` acceptance case present and no failures or errors.
+- Scenarios C/D (optional) behave as stated.
+- No tracked file outside the three in-scope paths changed.
