+# Feature Specification: Accept the MCP Standard Metadata Field in Handoff Tool Calls
+
+**Feature Branch**: `001-bench`
+
+**Created**: 2026-10-27
+
+**Status**: Draft
+
+**Input**: User description: "The task-handoff MCP stdio adapter (`plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py`, `--mcp-stdio`) refuses any `tools/call` whose `params` has a field other than `name` and `arguments`. Native MCP clients send the standard `_meta` object (for example `{\"progressToken\": ...}`), so every tool call from them fails before dispatch. Accept `_meta` when it is an object; every other unknown params field, the identity-override refusal and the oversized-frame refusal must keep working. The Codex companion (`plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/task_handoff.py`) ships the same file and must stay byte-identical. Cover the `_meta` call in `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/test_task_handoff.py`."
+
+## Summary
+
+Today the task-handoff server rejects a tool call the moment its request parameters carry anything besides the tool name and its arguments. Conforming native MCP clients routinely attach a small standard metadata object to every tool call, so from such a client **every** handoff tool fails before any work happens. This feature accepts that standard metadata object (treating it as opaque and dispatch-neutral) while keeping every other existing guardrail intact — unknown fields, non-object metadata, identity-override attempts, and oversized frames must all still be refused — and it ships to both deployed adapters from the one shared file so they stay identical.
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*
+
+### User Story 1 - Native MCP clients can call handoff tools without spurious rejection (Priority: P1)
+
+A native MCP client connects to the task-handoff server over stdio, completes the handshake, lists the available tools, and then invokes one of the handoff tools. Per the MCP protocol, the client attaches a standard per-call metadata object alongside the tool name and its arguments. As things stand, the server sees that extra metadata field, concludes the call carries an "unsupported field," and refuses it before doing any work — so none of the three handoff tools are usable from a mainstream client. After this change the metadata object is accepted and ignored for dispatch, so the call proceeds exactly as it would if the metadata were not there.
+
+**Why this priority**: Until this works, every tool call from a conforming native MCP client fails before dispatch, which makes the entire adapter effectively unusable from the clients most people run. Clearing this single gate restores the whole offering.
+
+**Independent Test**: Drive the stdio adapter with a valid handshake followed by a tool call whose parameters carry the tool name, its arguments, and an object-valued metadata field (for example one holding a progress token). Observe a successful, non-error tool result that is the same shape as the result for an equivalent call without metadata. Deliverable value: the client completes its intended handoff operation instead of getting a spurious failure.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a connected native MCP client, **When** it calls a handoff tool and the call's metadata field is a proper object (for example carrying a progress token), **Then** the tool executes normally and returns the same result it would return without the metadata field.
+2. **Given** a connected native MCP client, **When** it calls a handoff tool carrying a metadata object with no recognizable keys (an empty object), **Then** the tool executes normally and the empty metadata has no effect on dispatch or on the result.
+3. **Given** a metadata-bearing tool call that is otherwise valid, **When** it is processed, **Then** the presence of metadata does not change which tool runs nor how its arguments are read.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 2 - The relaxation is minimal; every existing guardrail still holds (Priority: P2)
+
+A maintainer defending the adapter's strictness verifies that accepting the metadata object did not loosen anything else. Unknown fields, badly typed metadata, identity-override attempts, and oversized frames must all still be refused, and none may cause any Runtime work when refused.
+
+**Why this priority**: The adapter's safety model depends on tight validation before it touches the Runtime. The fix must remove the false rejection without opening a new hole, so the guardrails are the second thing to prove.
+
+**Independent Test**: Run each previously-rejected scenario — a call with an unrecognized field, a call whose metadata value is not an object, a call that overrides identity through its tool arguments, and an oversized frame — and confirm each is still refused, with no Runtime side effect.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a tool call whose parameters include a field other than the tool name, the tool arguments, and the metadata object, **When** it is processed, **Then** it is rejected as invalid and no tool executes.
+2. **Given** a tool call whose metadata field is present but holds a value that is not an object (a string, number, list, or similar), **When** it is processed, **Then** the call is rejected as invalid before dispatch.
+3. **Given** a tool call that combines a valid metadata object with another unrecognized field, **When** it is processed, **Then** the call is still rejected because the extra field remains unrecognized.
+4. **Given** a tool call that tries to override the adapter's identity by passing an identity or host field inside the tool arguments, **When** it is processed, **Then** the call is rejected (the identity-override refusal still applies).
+5. **Given** a request frame larger than the allowed maximum size, **When** it is read, **Then** it is rejected as an invalid request, is not partially parsed into a second request, and no Runtime work is performed (the oversized-frame refusal still applies).
+
+---
+
+### User Story 3 - Both deployed adapters behave identically (Priority: P3)
+
+The operator maintains two deployments of the task-handoff adapter (the primary plugin and the Codex companion plugin) that intentionally ship the identical adapter file. The fix lands once in that shared file, so both deployments accept metadata and reject everything else in exactly the same way.
+
+**Why this priority**: Drift between the two deployments would reintroduce inconsistent behavior for different users of the same capability. Keeping them identical guarantees parity and removes an entire class of "works here, fails there" reports.
+
+**Independent Test**: Confirm the two adapter files are byte-for-byte identical after the change, and confirm that an identical metadata-bearing tool call sent to either deployment produces the same acceptance decision and the same result shape.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** the primary adapter and the Codex companion adapter after the change, **When** their adapter files are compared, **Then** they are byte-identical.
+2. **Given** an identical metadata-bearing tool call, **When** it is sent to either deployment, **Then** each produces the same acceptance decision and the same result shape.
+
+---
+
+### Edge Cases
+
+- **Empty metadata object**: accepted; dispatch is unaffected.
+- **Metadata object with arbitrary nested values**: accepted; its contents are ignored for dispatch.
+- **Metadata value that is not an object** (string, number, list, boolean, or null): rejected as invalid before dispatch.
+- **Valid metadata object plus another unknown field**: still rejected, because the extra field remains unrecognized — a valid metadata object does not excuse other unknowns.
+- **Metadata present on a method that is not a tool call** (handshake, ping, tool listing): behavior is unchanged; those operations already ignore parameter content.
+- **Oversized frame that also carries metadata**: the frame-size refusal takes precedence; the frame is rejected before any parameter inspection.
+- **A stream of many frames where some carry metadata and some do not**: each frame is handled independently; metadata never carries across frames or influences a later frame.
+- **Missing tool name or arguments on a metadata-bearing call**: handled by the ordinary tool-call rules (unknown tool, or missing required argument), independently of metadata handling.
+
+## Requirements *(mandatory)*
+
+### Functional Requirements
+
+- **FR-001**: A tool call whose parameters consist of the tool name and its arguments plus an optional metadata object MUST be accepted and dispatched exactly as if the metadata were absent; the metadata MUST NOT influence which tool runs nor how its arguments are interpreted.
+- **FR-002**: A tool call whose metadata field holds a value that is not an object MUST be rejected as invalid before dispatch, with no tool-side effect.
+- **FR-003**: A tool call whose parameters include any field other than the tool name, the tool arguments, and the metadata object MUST be rejected as invalid (the existing unknown-field guardrail is preserved).
+- **FR-004**: The identity-override refusal MUST be preserved: a tool call that attempts to override the adapter's identity through its tool arguments MUST still be rejected.
+- **FR-005**: The oversized-frame refusal MUST be preserved: a request frame exceeding the maximum message size MUST still be rejected as an invalid request, MUST NOT be partially consumed as a second request, and MUST cause no Runtime work.
+- **FR-006**: The Codex companion adapter MUST remain byte-identical to the primary adapter after the change, so both accept metadata and enforce every guardrail identically.
+- **FR-007**: All previously supported operations (server handshake, ping, tool listing, and tool calls that do not carry metadata) MUST behave identically to before the change.
+- **FR-008**: The acceptance of a metadata-bearing tool call MUST be exercised by a dedicated case in the task-handoff hook's contract test suite, running in the same process as the existing rejection cases, and the whole suite MUST pass.
+
+### Key Entities *(include if feature involves data)*
+
+- **Tool Call Request**: an inbound message naming one of the handoff tools, carrying that tool's arguments and optionally a standard per-call metadata object.
+- **Standard Metadata Object**: the optional per-call metadata that a conforming MCP client attaches to a tool call (for example, a progress token). Its contents are opaque to the adapter and require no interpretation beyond the value being an object.
+- **Adapter Identity**: the host/session identity the server process is configured with. It fixes who is acting and cannot be overridden by a caller through tool arguments.
+
+## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*
+
+### Measurable Outcomes
+
+- **SC-001**: 100% of tool calls from a conforming native MCP client that carry a properly formed metadata object are dispatched successfully across all three exposed tools (zero spurious "unsupported field" failures attributable to the metadata object).
+- **SC-002**: 0 regressions in rejection: an unrecognized field (other than the metadata object), a non-object metadata value, an identity-override attempt, and an oversized frame are each still refused, and none produces a tool-side effect or a partial parse.
+- **SC-003**: The two shipped adapters are byte-identical (100% content match) after the change.
+- **SC-004**: Zero observable behavior difference for any request that does not carry the metadata field (handshake, ping, tool listing, and plain tool calls).
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- A "native MCP client" follows the Model Context Protocol revision pinned by the adapter and attaches its standard metadata field (`_meta`) to tool calls; the adapter treats the field's contents as opaque.
+- "Accepting the metadata" means ignoring it for dispatch: the adapter neither reads nor forwards it and validates it only to the extent of confirming the value is an object.
+- "Object" means a JSON object (a mapping); any other JSON type used for the metadata field is treated as invalid and rejected.
+- The relaxation is localized to the tool-call parameter validation; the dispatch logic, identity handling, and the frame-size limit are otherwise unchanged.
+- The primary and Codex companion adapters currently ship the identical file (verified as an equal content hash in this checkout), so applying the same edit to that shared file keeps them byte-identical; no drift is expected.
+- No new external dependency is introduced; the change is confined to the existing adapter and its existing contract tests.
+- Governance: no dedicated constitution file exists in this checkout, so the repository-root `AGENTS.md` is used as the governance reference for this feature; this substitution is recorded here per the command guidance.
